WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.560
So today, we're going to
continue our discussion

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.880
about reachability,
and then we move

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.680
on to the next
topic, which is that

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.400
of model predictive control.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.800
So as you remember
from last time,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.200
we discussed how a
particularly useful application

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.480
of continuous time
closed-loop optimal control

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.840
is represented by
reachability theory.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:33.480
That essentially involves
computing reachable sets

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.960
through the lens of the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.480
So we discussed last time
what reachable sets are

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.900
and what they represent.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.600
Depending on the interpretation
for the target set,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.140
we have two main instantiations
of the concept of reachable set,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:54.200
namely avoidance
set and reach sets.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.300
So what we're going to do today
is, based on these definitions,

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.270
see how we can apply the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.290
equation to compute these
mathematical objects.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.270
And then I will
discuss how, once you

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.350
have computed these
objects, you could use

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.370
those in a control pipeline.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.350
Any questions?

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:23.350
So just as a reminder, we
defined an avoidance set,

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.190
which is a particular
instantiation of a backward

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.990
reachable set, as the
set of states at time, t,

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.390
satisfying the
following definition.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.030
A stands for avoidance.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.070
This is the set of states for
which there exists a disturbance

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.830
such that for all
possible control,

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:47.110
the system enters the target
set at time t equal 0.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.590
And of course, once you
have computed this set,

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.550
you want to make sure that
you start outside of this set

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.550
to guarantee safety.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:59.100
For the opposite, the
reachable set is actually

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.660
the set of desirable
states, and is

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.060
defined as the set
of states at time, t,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.500
such that for all
disturbances, there

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.860
exists a control such
that the system enters

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.740
target set at time t equals 0.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.180
So in this case, you would like
to start within this set so that

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.660
no matter what nature
throws at you that there

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.540
is a control, the optimal
control, that will still

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.580
allow you to fulfill
your control objectives.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.980
So this naturally
become a constraint

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.800
in your trajectory
optimization problem.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.680
You want to ensure that in
the case of avoidance set,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.800
your trajectory stays
outside of that set.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.540
In the case of a
reach set, you want

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:47.060
to make sure that your initial
condition is within that set.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.300
Now the question is, how
we compute these sets?

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.260
So the point is that you can
use the Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.820
equation to compute these
sets in a very procedural way.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.140
Specifically, as we will
see, the computation

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.700
of the backward reachable
sets, both in the avoidance

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.420
case and the reach case, entails
solving a differential game

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.597
where the outcome is Boolean.

00:03:11.597 --> 00:03:13.180
What does it mean a
differential game?

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.540
From last time,
differential game

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.320
means that there
are two players.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.780
We are player 1.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.340
We use the controls
at our disposal

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.340
in order to either
avoid a target set

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.840
or reach a target set.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.500
And then there is
player 2, nature,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.820
that, through a
disturbance control,

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:35.980
is trying to thwart our
attempt to fulfill our control

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.740
objectives.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:42.380
Now, the question is, at
the level of a two-player

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:43.420
structure--

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.180
should be quite
intuitive that, actually,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.940
the Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs
formalism is well set up

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.540
to address these problems.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:53.050
The question, though, how do
we instantiate all the problem

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:56.633
formulation in a
way that is aligned

00:03:56.633 --> 00:03:58.550
with the definition of
backward reachable set,

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.770
particularly because
belonging to a set

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.370
or not is a binary condition?

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:10.130
When backward reachable set--
sorry, in the optimal control

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:14.310
formulation, we only framed it
in terms of a cost function.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:22.570
So the question is, can we
reframe membership to a set

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.150
through the lens
of a cost function

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.570
so that we can reduce everything
to the optimal control

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.010
formulation, to
which we can then

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:31.930
apply the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation?

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:36.650
And the short answer is, yes, we
can encode this Boolean outcome

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:40.810
of belonging or
not to target set

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.730
within the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.190
formulation by removing
the running cost.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:48.770
Since we only care about
being in the target

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.720
set at the final time or
not being in the final set

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:56.260
at the target time, there is
no notion of running cost.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:01.320
And by picking the final cost,
to denote set membership.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.240
The question is how we do that?

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:07.640
It turns out that this
is relatively easy

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:12.920
and leads to the level
set method for backward

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.120
reachable computations.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.400
Specifically,
remember the equations

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.200
that one needs to solve in order
to find the optimal control

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.860
profile in a differential game.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.200
In our problem, we
only care about getting

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:34.360
into the backward reachable
set at the final time.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:36.280
There is no notion
of a running cost.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.960
So accordingly, we set the
running cost, g, equal to 0

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.160
for the specific
case of computing

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.280
backward reachable set.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.230
So we are only left
with the final cost, h,

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.230
which shows up in the
boundary condition

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.510
for the partial
differential equation.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:59.190
Now, can we pick a final
cost that reflects the notion

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.550
that you either
belong to a target set

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.710
or you do not belong
to the target set?

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.930
And the answer is affirmative.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.550
So one can always
find a function h

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.590
of x such that set
t, the target set,

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:17.950
is equal to the
0 level set of h.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:28.270
That is, x belongs to a given
set t if and only if h of x

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:33.070
is less than or equal to 0.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.290
Let me give you an example.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.540
And through the example, you
see that this is actually

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:37.630
quite obvious.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.910
So let's assume that
the set, capital T,

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:48.860
is represented by a
circle with radius R.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:54.780
So T is defined as
the set of point

00:06:54.780 --> 00:07:02.740
x such that x squared plus
y squared is less than

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.100
or equal to R.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:11.460
Now, you can define
a function h that

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.380
is a function of--
so let's assume

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:15.760
that the state space of our
system is three-dimensional.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.460
So we have a unicycle
with state x.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.580
The longitudinal position
y, the lateral position.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.580
And theta, the heading.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:30.900
So we can define the function
h as h of xr yr theta r

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:34.280
as square root of--

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:47.890
so if your unicycle,
whose state space

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:53.290
is three-dimensional, x, y
position and heading, has xy

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:58.770
coordinates such that h is
negative, what is the unicycle?

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:04.750
We assume that we have a
reference frame like this one.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:12.469
If the function h is less than
or equal to 0, what is it?

00:08:12.469 --> 00:08:13.770
Inside the circle.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:14.630
Inside the circle.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:15.530
Exactly.

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:20.010
Because it means that the
distance is less than R.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:25.850
So if h less than or
equal to 0, it means--

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:27.970
x squared plus y
squared less than

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:32.610
or equal to R, which means that
its position, its displacement

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.250
from the origin, has
to be less than R.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:36.090
So it's inside the circle.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:40.409
If h is greater than 0, it
means that you are outside.

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:44.210
And of course, the more h
is negative means the deeper

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:45.570
you are in the circle.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:50.090
The more h is positive, the
farther you are from the circle.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:53.730
Actually, the magnitude h
also has an interpretation

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:57.370
in terms of how safe
or how unsafe you are.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:03.730
But most importantly,
the level set of h

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:07.370
tells you whether a system
is in the target set

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:10.810
or is not in the target set.

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:14.445
So then the idea of reframing
backward reachable set

00:09:14.445 --> 00:09:16.570
through the lens of
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:17.903
is fairly straightforward.

00:09:17.903 --> 00:09:19.070
You remove the running cost.

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:20.612
This is not a notion
on running cost.

00:09:20.612 --> 00:09:22.170
In computing backward
reachable sets,

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:26.330
you find a function
h that represents

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:31.730
belonging to a given set
for whatever target set, t,

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:32.710
you have defined.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.250
That's always possible.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:37.890
Of course, if you have something
that is a bit more complicated

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:39.412
than a circle, then
a function h is

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:40.870
going to be a bit
more complicated,

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:42.100
but that's not a problem.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.800
And thus, we have
reframed the problem

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:56.000
of avoidance of a target set
as the problem of maximizing

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:56.820
over u.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:59.020
We want to stay
outside of the set.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:00.160
So we want to make--

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.320
us, as player 1, we
want to make h positive

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:07.440
while the enemy, player 2,
wants to make h negative.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.480
And so we are in the
same exact formalism

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.760
of the Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs
problem,

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:16.780
but the difference is that
we only have the final cost.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.180
We don't have the running cost.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:22.200
Remember that for the
continuous time optimal control

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.300
formulation, the
final time is 0.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.460
So we start from some negative
time and the final time is 0.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:30.560
This is more of a convention.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:31.740
So this is an example.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.480
So this is an example
where the avoidance

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:38.240
set is this red circle.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.710
For this trajectory
here, the cost

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:46.230
is positive since
the final point

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.910
is outside of the
avoidance set, which

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:51.150
means that the h, the
final cost, is positive.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:56.710
And then opposite to that,
for this blue trajectory,

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:58.990
the final condition is
in the circle, which

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:03.230
means that h is negative, which
means that the overall cost is

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.510
negative.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:07.650
So we're not doing
anything deep here.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:10.220
We're just trying to find
a one-to-one correspondence

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.470
between the abstract problem
of backward reachable set

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:15.030
computation and the tool
that we know of, which is

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:16.610
that of Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs.

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:17.310
Yeah.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.230
[INAUDIBLE] show me how
safe you are? [? Will h ?]

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.110
always be more positive?

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:23.690
Yeah, yeah.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:27.230
So that's why, actually,
the magnitude of h

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:31.430
not only allows you to reflect
the problem of a reachable set

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:33.750
computation in the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.350
framework, but also gives you
some interesting additional

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:39.220
information, which is,
loosely speaking, a buffer.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.220
The information about
how much buffer you

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.040
have before crossing
the danger line,

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:45.640
which is when h is equal to 0.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:48.380
So short answer is yes.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:52.060
OK, so we're almost there.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:56.140
So this is the formulation
for an avoidance set.

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:58.460
So now that you want to--

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.140
so if you have a
problem where you

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:03.340
want to find a backward
reachable set, that is,

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:06.780
the set of initial conditions
that would lead you

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:11.500
to a collision-- so for your
set to enter some unsafe region

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:16.660
for some adversarial selection
of the [INAUDIBLE] profile,

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:20.740
the problem that you want
to solve is minimax problem.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:23.100
We already discussed
how to pick h.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.400
And then there is nothing
else that you have to do.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:29.940
You just write down the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:31.660
for this specific problem.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:33.000
Same as last time.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.140
We don't have the running cost.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.270
And of course, for the
boundary condition,

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:40.090
we use the h function
that represents membership

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:42.250
to a given set.

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:45.170
If instead you want to solve
the opposite problem, that

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:49.730
of reaching a goal-- so now
you want to enter into the set.

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:51.970
So you want to make h negative.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.290
And the player wants to
make-- the nature wants

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:56.250
to make h positive
because it doesn't

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:59.330
want you to go into the set.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:03.790
Then the problem formulation
is a swap in terms of minimax.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.410
And now, you want to minimize h.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:11.010
Player 2 wants to
maximize the cost.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:12.430
So all stays the same.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:15.290
It's just that the
max min and min max

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.610
are swapped in the conditions.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:19.430
So you would do the
same derivation,

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:21.590
but just swapping min max.

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:22.090
Yeah.

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:24.250
[INAUDIBLE] would
like to be used

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:27.810
to define a set such that you
can enter the feasible set?

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.770
Like iterate?

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:31.690
A set--

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.610
If you got reachable sets.

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:38.170
Right?

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:40.545
Yeah, I mean that's the notion
of backward reachable set.

00:13:40.545 --> 00:13:43.876
But by feasible set,
what do you mean exactly?

00:13:43.876 --> 00:13:45.370
[INAUDIBLE] course.

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:46.930
Yeah, of course.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.650
This will become then your
target set and then you can

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:51.290
propagate the dynamics, even--

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.570
yeah, yeah, of course.

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:56.772
And in general, we'll see--

00:13:56.772 --> 00:13:58.730
so I think your question
is pointing, actually,

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:02.770
to the next point I
want to make here,

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:07.650
which is the following, that
the formulation so far is

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:08.433
pretty static.

00:14:08.433 --> 00:14:09.850
So basically, I'm
telling you what

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:13.470
is the set of initial conditions
such that, at the end,

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:15.770
you are in a
desirable condition.

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:18.770
But in general, you
want to be desirable

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:21.450
along your full trajectory,
not necessarily just

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:25.970
at the endpoint, which
leads to the notion

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:28.610
of an evolution of the
concept of reachable

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:30.490
set into reachable tube.

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:32.380
Specifically, look at this case.

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:34.800
So here we have a case
where the trajectory stays

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:39.240
outside of the avoidance set.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.840
So the cost is positive.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:46.540
Here you have-- in particular
because the endpoint is outside.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.200
Here, the blue trajectory,
the endpoint is inside.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.760
So the cost is negative, OK?

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:55.120
But here, there is a case
whereby the trajectory

00:14:55.120 --> 00:15:00.440
passes through the avoidance set
for some time at t less than 0.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:01.740
But time t equals 0.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:05.680
So at the end, the state is
outside the avoidance set.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:09.200
So the cost, because of how we
formulated the problem before,

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.060
is negative.

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:13.400
So we deem this
trajectory as safe.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:15.500
Well, I mean it's
safe for the endpoint,

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:19.740
but there was a portion of time
where you were in a danger zone.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:21.480
Maybe this is a
zone where you were

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:22.980
too close to another aircraft.

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:26.120
So you might have
had a near-miss.

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:27.960
So the question
then is, how do we

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.910
make sure that we protect the
full trajectory from being

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:35.390
outside of an avoidance set, as
opposed to just the endpoint?

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:38.670
This leads to the notion
of a tube, whereby

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:41.750
we want to reason not just
with respect to the final time,

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:47.090
but across the
full time horizon.

00:15:47.090 --> 00:15:49.310
So basically, you're
building a safety tube

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:53.630
so that you stay your trajectory
and basically a tube outside it

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:56.590
outside of the avoidance set.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:16:01.790
Can we still use techniques from
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation?

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:04.790
The answer is, yes, we can.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:07.110
I will just give
you the intuition

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:10.590
here because this gets
a bit more convoluted.

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:13.270
But essentially, the
intuition is as follows.

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:16.550
So before setting
of reachable set,

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:22.510
you cared only about
the final endpoint.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:26.190
Now, you care about making sure
that your entire trajectory

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:28.740
stays out of the avoidance set.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:32.660
How do you change the
problem formulation such

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:34.780
that you encode this objective?

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:39.780
So one way that you can do
that is by replacing h of x0

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:46.380
as your cost with minimum
of tau in the optimization

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:49.140
horizon of h of x tau.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:52.380
So what is this saying is that
you are interested in making

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:57.700
sure that not just h
evaluated at x of 0

00:16:57.700 --> 00:17:00.740
is greater than 0, which
means that you're safe,

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:04.619
but that the minimum of h
across all of that trajectory

00:17:04.619 --> 00:17:06.140
is greater than 0.

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.380
Because if that is
the case, it means

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:12.740
that at every single point in
time, h evaluated at x of t

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:14.900
is greater than 0,
which means that you are

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:16.560
outside of the avoidance set.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:21.140
Make sense?

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:23.900
So basically, you are
strengthening or actually

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:27.432
making your cost a bit more
sophisticated to encode

00:17:27.432 --> 00:17:28.890
the notion that
you don't just care

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:32.430
about what happens at the end
of the optimization horizon,

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.590
but you care about what
happens throughout the horizon.

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:39.210
In particular, you
want to make sure that,

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:42.610
in the worst possible situation
across your optimization

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:48.210
horizon, h never dips below 0.

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:52.010
Now, this is a cost that
we haven't seen last time.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:55.210
So actually, it changes a little
bit the derivation that we did.

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.650
We're not going to
do the derivation,

00:17:57.650 --> 00:18:02.650
but basically, the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:05.490
takes this form that is
a bit more complicated

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:08.730
because now we have an
additional minimization.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:11.070
And I'm not going to dig
much deeper than that.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:15.170
My main point is that if
you care about making sure

00:18:15.170 --> 00:18:18.850
that your entire trajectory
stays out of danger,

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:21.490
there is actually a
formulation of optimal control

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:23.470
that allows you to
compute that object.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:24.890
It's a little bit
of a refinement

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:27.150
of the Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs
equation.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:29.370
We're not going to
go down this path

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:31.930
because we have many
other concepts to discuss

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:32.712
in this class.

00:18:32.712 --> 00:18:35.170
But if you're interested, I
provided you with the reference

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:35.697
to do so.

00:18:35.697 --> 00:18:37.530
My most important point
here is to make sure

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:39.327
that you are familiar
with this problem,

00:18:39.327 --> 00:18:41.410
and you know that there
are tools at your disposal

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:43.770
to solve it.

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:44.490
Yeah.

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:50.010
So both of BRS and BRT refer
to a set of initial states?

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:50.850
Yeah.

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:51.572
So what is the--

00:18:51.572 --> 00:18:53.030
What were the
different properties?

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:56.330
So here, you make sure that it's
a set of initial states such

00:18:56.330 --> 00:19:00.610
that you don't end up in danger
at the end of optimization

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:01.610
horizon.

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.410
This is the set
of initial states

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:05.410
such that throughout
optimization horizon,

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:08.130
you stay out of danger.

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:13.258
[INAUDIBLE] we'll be solving
for either the BRS or BRT?

00:19:13.258 --> 00:19:14.050
Or are we solving--

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:14.675
For two things.

00:19:14.675 --> 00:19:16.890
You are solving for
the BRS and you're

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:21.130
solving for the optimal
control profile, u.

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:26.440
So I mean, you start from
a state in the backward

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:27.840
reachable set, OK?

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:32.080
But if you don't use the
optimal control policy,

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.400
you might still-- so
actually, in the case

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:37.120
of the avoidance set, you
start with an initial condition

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.680
outside the backward
reachable set.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:42.160
But if you don't use the
optimal control policy,

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:44.760
actually, the disturbance
might still lead you

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:45.740
into the avoidance set.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:49.080
So you need both, both
the set and the associated

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:50.600
optimal control policy.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.560
But the optimal control
policy, once you

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.400
have solved for the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation,

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:57.540
can be computed quite readily.

00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:59.840
So this is an easy computation.

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:04.800
So once you compute the
backward reachable set,

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:09.960
you can also easily compute
the optimal control.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:11.560
Yeah.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:15.920
So what you were talking about
with reaching versus avoiding,

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:19.020
when you hear you switch
maximum to min max,

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:21.950
then it would be
a value function

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:23.850
in the backwards reachable tube.

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:24.870
Does the outside--

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:26.370
So here there are
two things, right?

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:30.450
So there is a avoid versus
reach and tube versus set.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:33.830
So this slide is only
about avoid versus reach.

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:36.070
There is no notion of tube here.

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:39.670
Is there any notion
of reaching for tubes?

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:44.790
Like if you want your agent to
pass through a certain corridor?

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:47.970
Yeah, there is, even though it's
slightly less commonly used.

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:53.910
So typically-- even though
I frame the problem also

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.750
from the point of view
of reach, typically,

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:58.430
reachability-based
computations are

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:00.670
used more for the
purposes of safety

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:02.630
to-- basically as
additional constraints

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:04.630
you have in your trajectory
optimization process

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:07.590
to make sure that you have
enough buffer to avoid

00:21:07.590 --> 00:21:09.430
collisions or stuff like that.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:13.610
So the most common formulation
is the avoidance safety one.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:18.370
So let me show you one example.

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:21.380
And of course, the set
of initial conditions

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:24.420
for which you're guaranteed
to stay out of danger

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:31.520
is that such that the value
function is greater than 0.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:39.660
Now, let me show you an example
to make these things a bit more

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:40.400
concrete.

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:41.740
Yes.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:45.380
Oh, are dynamics make it
such that we can never

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.060
reach our target set?

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.340
What happens when you're
trying to initialize

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:51.300
a condition in the target set?

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:53.940
Yeah, basically
your set is empty.

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:56.820
And either you
change the problem

00:21:56.820 --> 00:21:59.980
or you don't
control-- basically,

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:01.820
you don't deploy your robot.

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:04.040
But at least it gives
you a strong information.

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:07.420
It means that the way
you frame the problem,

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:09.620
maybe because you were
too conservative-- it

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:13.420
might be that your problem
formulation is too conservative.

00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:15.680
Maybe you don't have
enough control actuation,

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:17.770
so you want to spend
a bit more money

00:22:17.770 --> 00:22:20.470
on buying better actuators.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:22.960
But it's saying something
about your problem formulation.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:24.710
Maybe it's just how
you frame the problem.

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:28.830
Or maybe there is an inherent
limitation in your robot.

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:33.690
And then, for example, you
have to design a better robot,

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:35.530
have more control authority.

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:37.170
But, yeah.

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:38.470
So let me show you one example.

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:40.850
So this is actually
quite illustrative.

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:47.210
So we have a situation whereby
we have two airplanes modeled

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:48.710
with unicycle dynamics.

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:51.490
So the equations of motion
for the first airplanes

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:54.030
are x dot equal v
cosine of theta,

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:57.353
where dot is equal to v sine of
theta, theta dot is equal to u.

00:22:57.353 --> 00:22:58.770
So essentially,
the state space is

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:01.850
three-dimensional, xy
position and heading.

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.710
And you control
the heading theta.

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:10.890
So basically, the time rate
of change of the heading

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:14.200
through the control u.

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:20.960
And the vehicle goes at speed
v. Same equations of motion

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:23.920
for the other vehicle, x
dot equal v cosine of theta,

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:27.020
y dot equal v sine of
theta, theta dot equal ub.

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:32.620
So now, I pose
you this question.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.280
So what is the initial set of--

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:38.200
what is the set of
initial conditions joint?

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:42.520
So between aircraft A
and aircraft B. Such

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:46.760
that there is a
trajectory for aircraft B

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.760
that no matter how much we're
trying to avoid a collision,

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:52.200
may lead to a collision,
whereby by collision we

00:23:52.200 --> 00:24:01.910
mean that the relative position
between A and B is in--

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:05.200
so the relative
distance is less than r.

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:07.160
So essentially,
our avoidance set

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:14.960
is, indeed, a circle in
the relative position xa

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:17.720
minus xb and ya minus yb.

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:21.320
We want the relative distance
between the two aircraft

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:23.080
to be less than r.

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:30.160
So if we center everything
around aircraft A,

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:35.820
want to make sure that B is
never inside this kind of shape

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:36.460
of circle.

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:43.000
So what is the set of
initial conditions that

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:46.400
allow us to make sure that
a collision, or at least

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:49.800
a near-miss, never happens?

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:53.200
So we are exactly in the setting
of computing and avoidance

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:55.060
set and associated
control profile

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:59.240
for player A, which is
us, to avoid a collision.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:02.400
So I posted a script.

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:03.580
Is the problem clear?

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:08.215
Any question about
the problem set?

00:25:08.215 --> 00:25:09.340
I mean, this is real stuff.

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:11.990
I mean, how people
may want to implement

00:25:11.990 --> 00:25:16.510
some level of collision
avoidance for aircraft.

00:25:16.510 --> 00:25:23.790
So I implemented the
Hamilton-Jacobi reachability

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:24.530
computation.

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:28.030
So this is useful because it
gives you a reference codebase

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:30.270
to do this type of computation.

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:34.210
But here, in particular, I
want to show the main result.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:38.510
So I implemented the
dynamics of these systems.

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:40.230
So this is not too important.

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:43.176
I will just highlight here.

00:25:43.176 --> 00:25:45.590
You know, it's a bit small.

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:48.910
That basically, the terminal
set, the terminal values,

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:54.110
is defined as a
circle with radius 5.

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:58.390
So that's where I
implement the h function.

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:00.910
So I call the package--
we called it up in my lab.

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:02.830
Called HJ reachability.

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:08.510
This is essentially the end
result of an HJ computation.

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:10.180
So what is this thing saying?

00:26:10.180 --> 00:26:12.860
It's a weird shape.

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:19.140
So x represents in
relative coordinate

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:21.760
the relative longitudinal
displacement.

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:29.380
So if this is
aircraft, aircraft--

00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:34.540
so this distance
here would be x.

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:39.500
This distance here would be y.

00:26:39.500 --> 00:26:43.660
So what we have here-- and
z is the relative heading

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:45.580
between the two aircraft.

00:26:45.580 --> 00:26:47.080
OK?

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:53.700
So this is the 0-level set of
the cost function as computed

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:56.540
through Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs
equation,

00:26:56.540 --> 00:27:02.220
which means that for those
set of states for which j is

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:09.570
negative, namely, you are
within these green shape,

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:11.310
then you might end
up in a collision.

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:14.930
If you're outside, you
don't end up in a collision.

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:16.930
Why this weird shape?

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:20.170
So to illustrate how to
interpret this result--

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:22.890
so this is basically
the set of states

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:26.290
for which j is equal to 0.

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:34.330
Assume that z, the
relative heading, is--

00:27:39.116 --> 00:27:41.270
the relative heading is 0.

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:50.910
So basically, the
aircraft are aligned.

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:59.090
So, if this is the case, then
in order to be out of danger,

00:27:59.090 --> 00:28:02.090
it's just the aircraft
can follow each other,

00:28:02.090 --> 00:28:04.330
either they start
outside of the circle

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:06.340
and they will stay outside
the circle forever.

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:07.980
Or if they are
inside the circle,

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:09.720
we just say inside
the circle forever,

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:11.880
and they are in a
dangerous situation.

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:16.880
So that's why the backward
reachable set is simply

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:22.080
the shape of your avoidance set.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:25.600
But let's see what happens
when z, the relative heading,

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:28.860
is close to 3, which
basically means pi.

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:36.240
So it takes a little
bit actually of practice

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:40.200
to view all of this, but then
it becomes quite intriguing.

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:41.980
Now, why it's not
picking this up?

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:50.900
So this is the part where
I had to show my computer.

00:28:50.900 --> 00:28:57.280
It's very difficult to show
this shape on the board.

00:28:57.280 --> 00:28:57.960
Yeah.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:01.480
So let me try to
make it a bit bigger.

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:04.040
So say that-- ugh.

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:07.760
Told you that is a
little bit tricky.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:08.620
Oh, come on.

00:29:12.680 --> 00:29:19.240
So say that your pi equals
3 so that the aircraft are

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:20.460
opposite of each other.

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:43.460
And-- so this axis is still
the longitudinal displacement.

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:45.200
So what is it saying?

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:50.400
That if you are with a positive
longitudinal displacement,

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:54.760
namely the aircraft--
so this is aircraft A.

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:57.280
So aircraft B is here.

00:29:57.280 --> 00:30:01.790
In order to ensure
collision avoidance,

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:07.150
initially, the aircraft
had to be more separated.

00:30:07.150 --> 00:30:09.790
Because the other aircraft
is just pointing at you.

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:12.310
So you need a little bit more
maneuvering space in order

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:14.510
to avoid the other aircraft.

00:30:14.510 --> 00:30:19.733
If instead the aircraft is
beneath you, then no problem.

00:30:19.733 --> 00:30:21.150
The only thing
that you care about

00:30:21.150 --> 00:30:22.950
is that you don't
start in a situation

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:25.110
where you are already
within the circle,

00:30:25.110 --> 00:30:27.230
but then otherwise,
you can just escape.

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:29.910
So that's why you have
this kind of protrusion

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:35.357
in this direction to take into
account about the geometric part

00:30:35.357 --> 00:30:36.690
and dynamic part of the problem.

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:37.630
Does that make sense?

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:39.630
So this is just saying
something obvious, right,

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:45.090
that if the two aircraft
are on a collision course,

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.550
you need to have more
lateral separation.

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:51.830
That's what this
blob here is saying.

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:54.030
It's kind of nice that
we computed, actually,

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:55.430
from first principle.

00:30:55.430 --> 00:30:59.900
And we know exactly for
then each possible value

00:30:59.900 --> 00:31:02.340
of the relative
heading by how much you

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:04.040
want to ensure
lateral separation,

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:06.360
also take into account--
longitudinal separation.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.840
Also taking into account
about lateral separation.

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:12.820
So now I can tell you exactly
how much longitudinal separation

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:15.940
you should have if you start
from this initial condition,

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:18.100
or if you start from
initial condition,

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:20.500
or if you start from
initial condition.

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:22.780
Of course, this one,
from the point of view

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:26.040
of avoiding collision
is worse than this one.

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:27.780
And this is actually
reflecting this kind

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:31.280
of non-symmetrical
shape of this object.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:31.980
Yeah.

00:31:31.980 --> 00:31:34.160
Assuming the same
constant velocity.

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:35.740
Yes, v, yeah.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:38.740
Otherwise, that-- you can also
choose two different velocities?

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:39.560
Of course.

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:43.220
The shape of the
thing will change.

00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:45.220
Just to reiterate
on the concept.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:52.380
So here, I'm showing how the
projection of the backward

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:57.100
reachable set changes depending
on how long the time horizon is.

00:31:57.100 --> 00:32:02.610
For now, let's just look
at the black curve here.

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:04.210
Blue means negative.

00:32:04.210 --> 00:32:06.098
I mean, darker blue
means negative.

00:32:06.098 --> 00:32:07.390
Everything else means positive.

00:32:07.390 --> 00:32:10.170
So here is the scale.

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:13.370
So this is essentially the
projection of that weird thing

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:16.690
that was three-dimensional
on a 2D space,

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:20.410
where we fix the
relative heading at 3.7.

00:32:20.410 --> 00:32:22.290
So it'd be higher than pi.

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:25.050
x, still notion of
longitudinal distance. y,

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:27.570
notion of lateral distance.

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:31.890
So we're fixing the relative
heading at a little bit more

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:32.550
than pi.

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:36.210
So it's basically
something like that.

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:38.570
So the vehicle is
pointing-- the vehicle

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:40.810
B is pointing in this
direction, and then you

00:32:40.810 --> 00:32:43.570
want to understand where
it should be placed

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:46.450
at the beginning to
make sure that we do not

00:32:46.450 --> 00:32:48.490
incur a collision.

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:52.510
So fix the longitudinal
separation equal to 5.

00:32:56.040 --> 00:33:03.120
So if-- and my point is, why,
basically, the reachable set

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:06.520
has the shape that
is a bit tilted?

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:11.400
Well, if you fix the
longitudinal separation at 5,

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:20.320
then do you want to add more
lateral buffer if the vehicle--

00:33:20.320 --> 00:33:25.160
so we're fixing the
longitudinal separation, right?

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:28.560
So the question is, do you
want to add more lateral safety

00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:33.240
buffer if the vehicle starts
with a negative lateral distance

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:35.680
or with a positive
lateral distance?

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:38.800
In other words, do you think
that this situation is safer?

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:40.220
Or this situation is safer?

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:47.120
Who votes for option 1?

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:49.120
Who votes for option 2?

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:50.320
Great.

00:33:50.320 --> 00:33:52.440
Happy to fly with all of you.

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:55.855
So yeah, of course, because this
guy, in order to really collide

00:33:55.855 --> 00:33:58.230
with us, will need to do a
much more complicated maneuver

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:01.550
and we have much
more space to evade.

00:34:01.550 --> 00:34:04.570
So that's why now the
reachable set is tilted.

00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:07.950
It's simply saying
that if you are

00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:11.330
in-- you start with a
negative lateral distance,

00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:13.230
you need less buffer.

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.449
With a positive distance,
you need more buffer.

00:34:15.449 --> 00:34:20.630
You need up to 5, say, meters.

00:34:20.630 --> 00:34:24.350
What it's saying-- why is this
thing evolving through time?

00:34:24.350 --> 00:34:26.570
The shape of the
reachable set, of course,

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:29.290
depends on the initial time.

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:31.909
So if you are computing
the backward reachable set

00:34:31.909 --> 00:34:35.870
from the perspective of
time equal minus 2.5,

00:34:35.870 --> 00:34:39.150
minus 3, minus 3.5, remember
that the final time is always

00:34:39.150 --> 00:34:41.630
0 for these computations.

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:47.150
So it turns out that after
3.5 units of time, actually,

00:34:47.150 --> 00:34:49.810
the backward reachable
set goes to a constant.

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:53.489
So after minus 4,
minus 5, minus 6,

00:34:53.489 --> 00:34:56.190
you always get the same
backward reachable set.

00:34:56.190 --> 00:34:57.170
Why is that the case?

00:34:57.170 --> 00:34:59.530
Is that in most
of these problems,

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:02.690
where things are stationary,
after a transient,

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:07.110
then the problem setups into a
stationary situation, whereby

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:12.530
either the player 2 is just
chasing us or we have collided.

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:17.230
So it doesn't change that we
have more time at our disposal.

00:35:17.230 --> 00:35:20.870
Let me show you
some example of--

00:35:20.870 --> 00:35:23.710
so you do this computation, you
compute the control profiles,

00:35:23.710 --> 00:35:25.870
and then you start injecting
some possible initial

00:35:25.870 --> 00:35:26.950
conditions.

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:28.770
And here I picked three for fun.

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:30.770
So blue is player 1.

00:35:30.770 --> 00:35:32.510
Red is player 2.

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:37.190
So we want to make sure
the player 2 doesn't

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:39.710
enter our personal space.

00:35:39.710 --> 00:35:42.750
So here is a situation
where we started

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:47.830
in terms of relative coordinates
outside of the backward

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:49.030
reachable set.

00:35:49.030 --> 00:35:53.980
So no matter how hard player
2 is trying to play the game

00:35:53.980 --> 00:35:56.380
to catch with us, it can't.

00:35:56.380 --> 00:35:58.020
Velocity here is the same.

00:35:58.020 --> 00:35:59.660
You see that after
a while, then you're

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:00.960
just going to steady state.

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:04.020
Additional time doesn't
really buy you anything.

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:09.940
So this is a situation where
we started with player 2

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:12.120
essentially inside
the personal space,

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:13.780
and then it just
locks in, and then it

00:36:13.780 --> 00:36:17.660
stays in our personal
space throughout.

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:20.300
Here is an interesting
case that I found,

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:29.100
whereby the player 2,
for a little while,

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:32.060
transitioned in our
backward reachable set.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:35.700
Sorry, in our protected space.

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:37.700
But then we're able
to maneuver out,

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:41.380
and then the other player
is unable to catch with us.

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:45.780
So for the way we frame this
problem, we have succeeded.

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:49.090
In reality, we should
care about the fact

00:36:49.090 --> 00:36:51.850
that for some small
portion of time, actually,

00:36:51.850 --> 00:36:54.950
player 2 enter our
personal space.

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:56.330
So this is undesirable.

00:36:56.330 --> 00:36:58.530
So this is motivation
for actually switching

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:00.850
to a backward reachable
tube formulation, which

00:37:00.850 --> 00:37:02.070
is not what we're doing here.

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:03.690
So that's why for
this computation,

00:37:03.690 --> 00:37:05.195
actually, this is
a success story,

00:37:05.195 --> 00:37:06.570
but in reality,
probably we would

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:08.670
like to use a backward
reachable tube formulation.

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:12.210
In which case, this would
be a negative story.

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:15.930
So what I'm doing here is just
to initialize the two vehicles

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:18.890
inside or outside the
backward reachable set,

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:21.090
playing the game
optimally, as derived

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:24.290
through the solution of the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation,

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:28.170
and then displaying
the trajectories.

00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:30.050
Isn't this cool?

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:32.970
I think it's nice.

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:34.770
And people use this
stuff for real.

00:37:34.770 --> 00:37:40.090
So for example, in the--

00:37:40.090 --> 00:37:43.810
in several production-grade
autopilots for cars,

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:47.560
you have something reminiscent
of a backwards reachable set

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:52.240
to ensure that at the lowest
level of safety, no matter what

00:37:52.240 --> 00:37:55.400
another driver might
do close to you,

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:58.740
you still have a safety buffer
so that you can maneuver it out.

00:37:58.740 --> 00:37:59.620
Yeah.

00:37:59.620 --> 00:38:02.500
Is it guaranteed to
converge for some?

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:03.840
The backward reachable tube?

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:05.200
So first of all, here
we're not computing

00:38:05.200 --> 00:38:06.200
backward reachable tube.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:06.720
Oh.

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:08.440
We are computing
backward reachable sets.

00:38:08.440 --> 00:38:11.240
In general, for a
time-varying formulation,

00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:13.060
it converges to a
steady-state solution.

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:19.540
I think it's pretty neat.

00:38:19.540 --> 00:38:21.720
I know that mathematically,
probably this was

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:25.200
the most sophisticated lecture.

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:29.280
I tried to give you as
much intuition as I could.

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:33.460
The treatment of differential
games, mathematically speaking,

00:38:33.460 --> 00:38:36.120
is quite involved.

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:38.880
But I do hope that at least
you have an understanding

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:43.040
of the main ideas about what
is possible with this formalism

00:38:43.040 --> 00:38:47.150
and also what are the underlying
algorithmic packages that

00:38:47.150 --> 00:38:49.270
allow you to--

00:38:49.270 --> 00:38:52.510
algorithm concepts and
packages that allow

00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:54.410
you to solve these problems.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:01.550
And speaking about
algorithmic packages,

00:39:01.550 --> 00:39:03.830
how do you solve this stuff?

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:07.910
Well, solving a partial
differential equation is hard.

00:39:07.910 --> 00:39:11.030
Actually, the
example that I gave

00:39:11.030 --> 00:39:12.898
you is based on a
package we developed

00:39:12.898 --> 00:39:15.190
in my lab, which is one of
the fastest packages written

00:39:15.190 --> 00:39:17.230
in Python to solve
Hamilton-Jacobi reachability

00:39:17.230 --> 00:39:18.150
equations.

00:39:18.150 --> 00:39:20.070
So you could use that.

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:23.910
That said, typically, you
can solve these problems

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:28.310
for maybe five-dimensional state
space, six-dimensional state

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:31.590
space, and then things
break down because

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:33.890
of the curse of dimensionality.

00:39:33.890 --> 00:39:40.730
So the solvers don't scale
beyond that dimensionality.

00:39:40.730 --> 00:39:43.230
That said, it doesn't mean that
you can't solve reachability

00:39:43.230 --> 00:39:45.570
a problem with more than
five or six dimensions.

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:48.110
You just have to resort
to approximations.

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:51.630
And this is still a fairly
active field of research.

00:39:51.630 --> 00:39:54.670
There are many different
ways of breaking

00:39:54.670 --> 00:39:58.030
this scalability barrier
by using approximations

00:39:58.030 --> 00:40:01.590
through neural networks,
sampling-based methods,

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:04.270
giving up some guarantees of
accuracy, and so on and so

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:05.430
forth.

00:40:05.430 --> 00:40:07.330
So I won't go into
the details here,

00:40:07.330 --> 00:40:09.910
but at least you have
an exact implementation

00:40:09.910 --> 00:40:12.150
of the Hamilton-Jacobi
reachability computations

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:15.150
through the HJ package
that I showed you before.

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:17.830
And then just for
you to know, there

00:40:17.830 --> 00:40:20.378
are a number of techniques
that are approximate in nature

00:40:20.378 --> 00:40:22.670
that allow you to solve this
problem beyond five or six

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:25.590
dimensions.

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:28.910
All right, let's move to MPC.

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:30.330
Any questions in the meantime?

00:40:36.390 --> 00:40:38.110
Comparatively
speaking, MPC is going

00:40:38.110 --> 00:40:41.460
to be a walk in the park
in terms of complexity.

00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:44.900
MPC is actually very--

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:46.820
it's very intuitive in general.

00:40:46.820 --> 00:40:49.560
It just feels right, I
think, as a control strategy,

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:51.740
as you'll see.

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:53.200
Going back to roadmap.

00:40:53.200 --> 00:40:56.200
So we spent quite a bit of time
on open-loop optimal control.

00:40:56.200 --> 00:41:00.220
We spent quite a bit of time
on closed-loop optimal control.

00:41:00.220 --> 00:41:03.380
This is fast, but less powerful
because we reason in terms

00:41:03.380 --> 00:41:05.620
of open-loop control sequences.

00:41:05.620 --> 00:41:09.860
This is powerful, we reason in
terms of closed-loop policies,

00:41:09.860 --> 00:41:13.260
but computationally complex both
in discrete-time formulations

00:41:13.260 --> 00:41:15.558
through the discrete-time
Bellman equation

00:41:15.558 --> 00:41:17.100
or in the continuous
time formulation

00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:21.020
through Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman
and Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs.

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:23.420
Can we get the best
of both worlds?

00:41:23.420 --> 00:41:26.700
Basically, the speed
of an open-loop

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:32.140
control method with the power
of a closed-loop control

00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:33.500
methodology.

00:41:33.500 --> 00:41:35.860
The answer is, yes,
to a large extent,

00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:39.610
through the lens of MPC,
Model Predictive Control.

00:41:39.610 --> 00:41:45.410
MPC is really a framework
more than a specific control

00:41:45.410 --> 00:41:46.570
strategy.

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:50.250
That said, I would say
that after PID controllers,

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:52.290
it's probably the most
widely-used control

00:41:52.290 --> 00:41:54.410
methodology out there.

00:41:54.410 --> 00:41:59.650
Originally developed in the '70s
and '80s more as a heuristic,

00:41:59.650 --> 00:42:02.770
primarily in the chemical
engineering domain.

00:42:02.770 --> 00:42:04.810
You may wonder why
chemical engineering.

00:42:04.810 --> 00:42:08.010
Because, two reasons.

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:09.690
First, there was a need.

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:11.772
You want to reason
about constraints.

00:42:11.772 --> 00:42:13.230
Typically, in
chemical engineering,

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:16.290
you don't want the
temperature of the products

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:18.890
that you're mixing
to go out of whack.

00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:21.310
So you really care
about constraints.

00:42:21.310 --> 00:42:23.970
Otherwise, the entire
thing blows up.

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:25.530
And constraints
are difficult to be

00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:30.450
dealt with directly through
frequency-based controls.

00:42:30.450 --> 00:42:32.970
But at the same time,
chemical processes typically

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:35.720
evolve on a slower timescale.

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:37.440
Multiple seconds.

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:41.800
So even though MPC relies
on, oftentimes, online

00:42:41.800 --> 00:42:43.280
optimization--

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:46.400
and at the time, the computers
were not particularly fast.

00:42:46.400 --> 00:42:48.720
People could still solve
those optimization problems

00:42:48.720 --> 00:42:51.520
at the level of a few seconds.

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:53.060
So it was just a sweet spot.

00:42:53.060 --> 00:42:53.940
There was a need.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:56.200
And actually, the
problem itself was

00:42:56.200 --> 00:42:59.760
well-attuned to solve
optimization problems at maybe

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:03.760
0.5 hertz or 0.1 hertz.

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:05.480
So what we're going
to do today is

00:43:05.480 --> 00:43:09.280
to introduce the
basic ideas behind MPC

00:43:09.280 --> 00:43:11.480
and start developing
a bit of theory

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:15.680
about how to apply MPC
in a principled way.

00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:19.280
Good readings about
MPC are the textbooks

00:43:19.280 --> 00:43:21.600
from Borrelli,
Bemporad, and Morari

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:25.760
and the textbook from
Rawlings, and Mayne, and Diehl.

00:43:25.760 --> 00:43:29.760
These scientists,
in general, have

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:32.440
been quite influential
in setting up

00:43:32.440 --> 00:43:35.870
the algorithmic
foundations of MPC.

00:43:35.870 --> 00:43:39.350
Until the early '90s, MPC
was used mostly in heuristic.

00:43:39.350 --> 00:43:44.430
And then people in the '90s and
2000 started developing a theory

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:51.270
about MPC that allow researchers
and developers to deploy MPC

00:43:51.270 --> 00:43:55.630
with mathematical assurances
about the correctness

00:43:55.630 --> 00:43:57.310
of the design.

00:43:57.310 --> 00:44:00.830
Most recently, as you
know, there have been a lot

00:44:00.830 --> 00:44:04.630
of advantages in terms of--
advances in terms of online

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:05.990
solvers--

00:44:05.990 --> 00:44:09.270
so this has been the main
trend, to really push MPC

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:13.390
into the control loop
with increasingly more

00:44:13.390 --> 00:44:15.990
stringent requirements
in terms of replanning

00:44:15.990 --> 00:44:19.410
rates, 10 hertz, 30
hertz, kilohertz,

00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:21.710
and so on and so forth.

00:44:21.710 --> 00:44:22.770
So what is MPC?

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:26.190
The idea of MPC
is really simple.

00:44:26.190 --> 00:44:28.990
And as I said, very natural.

00:44:28.990 --> 00:44:32.980
So the underlying idea is that
you have an infinite horizon

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:34.400
optimal control problem.

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:36.320
You want to solve it optimally.

00:44:39.220 --> 00:44:41.340
But that is very expensive.

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:44.900
And so you're fine with
solving it sub-optimally

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:48.100
but still in closed-loop form.

00:44:48.100 --> 00:44:50.300
So what does MPC do?

00:44:50.300 --> 00:44:55.420
MPC measures and
keeps, basically track,

00:44:55.420 --> 00:45:00.260
of previous values
of the states.

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:03.180
Based on that information,
based on information

00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:07.820
about the dynamics, based on the
controlling state constraints,

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:11.500
it computes a control profile.

00:45:11.500 --> 00:45:13.660
Here, we assume
that we are solving

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:15.000
the problem in discrete time.

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:19.660
So essentially,
solves-- it solves

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:22.420
an open-loop optimal
control problem

00:45:22.420 --> 00:45:27.220
over a finite horizon between
time, t, at time t plus Np.

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:36.100
You find that your
open-loop control sequence,

00:45:36.100 --> 00:45:43.540
along with a predicted sequence
of states or outputs that stem

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:48.900
from the application of your
optimized control sequence

00:45:48.900 --> 00:45:52.220
to the dynamics of your system--

00:45:52.220 --> 00:45:56.660
so, so far, this is just an
open-loop optimal control.

00:45:56.660 --> 00:46:06.340
But then what MPC does is to
use only the first control that

00:46:06.340 --> 00:46:10.020
makes the system
transition to the new state

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:12.500
or output at time t plus 1.

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:15.780
And then you completely
discard everything else

00:46:15.780 --> 00:46:18.860
that you computed at a
previous planning cycle,

00:46:18.860 --> 00:46:21.820
and you recompute
everything from scratch,

00:46:21.820 --> 00:46:26.980
but now starting at a new
state at time t plus 1.

00:46:26.980 --> 00:46:32.450
With the idea that at every
single planning cycle,

00:46:32.450 --> 00:46:36.530
you always compute-- you always
solve for an open-loop control

00:46:36.530 --> 00:46:38.170
problem.

00:46:38.170 --> 00:46:41.250
But since you
replan, which means

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:45.690
that you get to observe a
new state, and based on that,

00:46:45.690 --> 00:46:49.750
you solve a new open-loop
optimal control problem,

00:46:49.750 --> 00:46:56.130
you actually endow
the entire framework

00:46:56.130 --> 00:46:59.090
with the notion of
closed-loop because you

00:46:59.090 --> 00:47:01.490
are closing the loop on
your most recent observation

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:03.330
on the state.

00:47:03.330 --> 00:47:06.490
But you're always solving an
open-loop control problem.

00:47:06.490 --> 00:47:09.290
Turns out that this is really
a sweet spot between doing

00:47:09.290 --> 00:47:12.170
open-loop optimal
control and actually

00:47:12.170 --> 00:47:14.490
using closed-loop information
from the measurement

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:16.570
of the state.

00:47:16.570 --> 00:47:17.650
It seems wasteful, right?

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:23.035
Because you solve the problem
over maybe a long horizon,

00:47:23.035 --> 00:47:24.410
and then everything
that you have

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:29.960
computed after the first time
step goes into the trash.

00:47:29.960 --> 00:47:31.680
Not always, because
you can always

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:36.340
use this information to inform
the solution at the next step.

00:47:36.340 --> 00:47:39.100
But in its simplest
vanilla form,

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:41.200
you put everything
into the trash.

00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:44.600
There are some nuances,
like, for example, you

00:47:44.600 --> 00:47:49.680
may want to compute your
optimal control sequences up

00:47:49.680 --> 00:47:54.200
until end horizon t plus Nm
but keep your predictions

00:47:54.200 --> 00:47:55.680
going for a little bit further.

00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:58.380
So that there are a lot of
variations in the theme.

00:47:58.380 --> 00:48:00.100
So MPC is a framework.

00:48:00.100 --> 00:48:02.120
It's not a specific
control algorithm.

00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:05.800
But the main idea is
that you do receding

00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:08.800
horizon optimization,
always moving,

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:13.020
which again, is very natural,
is also what we do as humans.

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:17.520
We optimize our control
profile, then we

00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:20.320
walk around a little bit, of
course, we gather information,

00:48:20.320 --> 00:48:22.280
and we revise our plan.

00:48:22.280 --> 00:48:25.310
So the idea shouldn't
be too surprising.

00:48:25.310 --> 00:48:27.950
Does it make sense?

00:48:27.950 --> 00:48:30.830
There is a little bit of
notation here, t plus 1

00:48:30.830 --> 00:48:33.270
plus k given t plus 1.

00:48:33.270 --> 00:48:36.630
I will explain this
terminology in a second.

00:48:36.630 --> 00:48:37.910
So key ideas.

00:48:37.910 --> 00:48:40.350
At each sampling
time t, we solve

00:48:40.350 --> 00:48:43.830
an open-loop optimal control
problem over a finite horizon.

00:48:43.830 --> 00:48:47.350
How long the horizon should
be, we will discuss it later.

00:48:47.350 --> 00:48:49.630
We apply the
optimal input signal

00:48:49.630 --> 00:48:54.150
during the sampling
interval t, t plus 1.

00:48:54.150 --> 00:48:57.710
We throw into the
trash everything else.

00:48:57.710 --> 00:49:00.830
Then the system, under the
application of this input,

00:49:00.830 --> 00:49:04.110
evolves into the new
state at time t plus 1.

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:06.350
And now, at time
step t plus 1, we

00:49:06.350 --> 00:49:08.550
solve a new optimal
control problem

00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:12.090
based on new measurements of
the state of a shifted horizon.

00:49:12.090 --> 00:49:15.330
So now the horizon goes
one step into the future.

00:49:19.790 --> 00:49:24.260
So let me give you a little
bit more concrete formulation,

00:49:24.260 --> 00:49:29.460
particularly in the
setting of linear dynamics.

00:49:29.460 --> 00:49:32.980
So we consider the problem
again of regulating

00:49:32.980 --> 00:49:37.780
to the origin, the discrete-time
linear time-invariant system,

00:49:37.780 --> 00:49:41.020
x t plus 1 equals Ax plus Bu.

00:49:41.020 --> 00:49:42.580
So very similar
to what you would

00:49:42.580 --> 00:49:46.020
do with the classical
frequency domain control.

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:47.365
But we also have constraints.

00:49:47.365 --> 00:49:48.740
So this is not
something that you

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:50.657
can do with classical
frequency-based control.

00:49:50.657 --> 00:49:55.660
So we assume that the state
x belongs to some capital set

00:49:55.660 --> 00:49:58.940
X. So the set of safe states.

00:49:58.940 --> 00:50:02.245
And u belongs to
some capital set U.

00:50:02.245 --> 00:50:03.620
And here, we're
assuming that the

00:50:03.620 --> 00:50:06.940
sets X and U are
polyhedra, that is,

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:11.140
the sets that can be defined
by finitely many linear

00:50:11.140 --> 00:50:12.540
inequalities.

00:50:12.540 --> 00:50:16.860
So basically, half planes that
defines your feasible state

00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:19.604
space.

00:50:19.604 --> 00:50:20.580
A cube, for example.

00:50:20.580 --> 00:50:23.380
You're are confined
within the cube.

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:28.020
So what is the basic
formulation of MPC then?

00:50:28.020 --> 00:50:35.580
Assume that we can measure
fully the state x of t.

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:38.660
Then at each
replanning time at t,

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:41.580
we solve an optimal
control problem

00:50:41.580 --> 00:50:45.900
in open-loop in terms of
the variables ut, ut plus 1,

00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:50.187
all the way to ut plus N minus
1, to minimize an additive cost.

00:50:50.187 --> 00:50:52.520
But now, you should be very
familiar with the structure.

00:50:52.520 --> 00:50:56.260
So you have a terminal cost
plus the summation of a running

00:50:56.260 --> 00:51:00.320
cost, subject to the
dynamics state constraints

00:51:00.320 --> 00:51:02.220
control constraints.

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:04.100
The initial condition
has to be xt,

00:51:04.100 --> 00:51:06.180
the state you are in right now.

00:51:06.180 --> 00:51:08.100
That's how you close the loop.

00:51:08.100 --> 00:51:11.160
And also potentially adding
some terminal constraints.

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:14.580
So you want to make sure
that your predicted state

00:51:14.580 --> 00:51:19.970
after N steps belong to
some desirable set x of f.

00:51:19.970 --> 00:51:23.950
I will discuss later why we may
want to add this constraint.

00:51:27.130 --> 00:51:30.150
So you solve for this
optimal control sequence.

00:51:30.150 --> 00:51:31.590
You use the first term.

00:51:31.590 --> 00:51:33.305
You move into state xt plus 1.

00:51:33.305 --> 00:51:33.930
You measure it.

00:51:33.930 --> 00:51:37.050
And then you replan.

00:51:37.050 --> 00:51:40.410
Where is all this complicated
formulation here coming from?

00:51:40.410 --> 00:51:42.430
This vertical bar t and so on?

00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:45.130
What does that mean?

00:51:45.130 --> 00:51:46.510
So that means the following.

00:51:46.510 --> 00:51:52.890
So xt plus k given t
use this terminology.

00:51:52.890 --> 00:51:57.050
It's the state vector at
time t plus k predicted

00:51:57.050 --> 00:51:59.930
at time t, the system dynamics.

00:51:59.930 --> 00:52:03.370
ut plus k given t is
the input u at time t

00:52:03.370 --> 00:52:05.690
plus k computed at time t.

00:52:05.690 --> 00:52:08.930
Note that the state
predicted a step

00:52:08.930 --> 00:52:13.410
3 from the perspective of
time 1 is not necessarily

00:52:13.410 --> 00:52:17.840
the same state 3 predicted
from the perspective of time 2

00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:21.080
simply because maybe
disturbance might have perturbed

00:52:21.080 --> 00:52:22.460
our nominal trajectory.

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:28.020
Any questions here?

00:52:31.960 --> 00:52:35.800
So here, what I want to
highlight is these two guys.

00:52:35.800 --> 00:52:42.400
So these two guys
are additional terms

00:52:42.400 --> 00:52:50.160
that we inject in our MPC
formulation to regularize it.

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:55.860
Our optimal control formulation
basically has the running cost.

00:52:55.860 --> 00:52:57.920
It has the control constraints.

00:52:57.920 --> 00:53:01.080
Where are these determinant
constraints coming from?

00:53:01.080 --> 00:53:07.320
And what is this
auxiliary cost that

00:53:07.320 --> 00:53:12.720
is placed on the predictive
variable at time N coming from?

00:53:12.720 --> 00:53:16.590
So those two terms are actually
the two design choices or tuning

00:53:16.590 --> 00:53:18.790
knobs that, as
MPC designers, you

00:53:18.790 --> 00:53:26.030
can tune in order to optimize
the performance of an MPC

00:53:26.030 --> 00:53:27.550
control framework.

00:53:27.550 --> 00:53:30.930
So what we will see for
the rest of this lecture

00:53:30.930 --> 00:53:32.790
and then the
lecture on Wednesday

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:38.630
will be to see how do we
tune this auxiliary terminal

00:53:38.630 --> 00:53:44.590
constraint here, this terminal
cost here, and potentially

00:53:44.590 --> 00:53:45.970
the end.

00:53:45.970 --> 00:53:48.710
So how much lookahead
steps you have

00:53:48.710 --> 00:53:53.470
to make sure that you
have a good performance

00:53:53.470 --> 00:53:55.390
once you deploy MPC.

00:53:55.390 --> 00:53:58.070
And in general, you
care about two things.

00:53:58.070 --> 00:54:00.750
First of all, you care
about the so-called notion

00:54:00.750 --> 00:54:02.990
of a persistent feasibility.

00:54:02.990 --> 00:54:06.270
So you want to make sure
that once you transition

00:54:06.270 --> 00:54:09.390
into the new state,
the new optimal control

00:54:09.390 --> 00:54:13.260
problem that you're trying
to solve is still feasible.

00:54:13.260 --> 00:54:14.280
Otherwise, you're stuck.

00:54:14.280 --> 00:54:16.500
I mean, that's back to
your colleague's question

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:20.480
before, what happens when the
reachable set, for example,

00:54:20.480 --> 00:54:21.180
is empty?

00:54:21.180 --> 00:54:22.580
You don't want to end
up in that situation

00:54:22.580 --> 00:54:23.640
because it means
that you're stuck.

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:25.015
You have nothing
that you can do.

00:54:25.015 --> 00:54:26.540
Whatever you do,
basically, you're

00:54:26.540 --> 00:54:28.460
going to violate a constraint.

00:54:28.460 --> 00:54:30.340
So how do you make sure
that doesn't happen?

00:54:30.340 --> 00:54:32.780
So how do you play
with your tuning knobs

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:34.380
so that doesn't happen?

00:54:34.380 --> 00:54:37.780
And then of course, assuming
that doesn't happen, how do you

00:54:37.780 --> 00:54:40.220
make sure that you fulfill
your task, in this case,

00:54:40.220 --> 00:54:43.480
regulating the
state to the origin?

00:54:43.480 --> 00:54:45.360
So you basically
want x equal to 0.

00:54:48.180 --> 00:54:53.020
OK, so we talked
about the terminology.

00:54:53.020 --> 00:54:57.060
Before we address the two
questions I mentioned before,

00:54:57.060 --> 00:54:59.940
let me add a little bit more
notation, but not too much.

00:54:59.940 --> 00:55:04.820
So as I said, what
you do in MPC is

00:55:04.820 --> 00:55:09.660
you take the first control in
the optimal control sequence

00:55:09.660 --> 00:55:14.580
that you have computed, which
is a function of the state,

00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:22.420
because the first
predicted state

00:55:22.420 --> 00:55:25.880
has to be equal to the current
state, x of t, of course.

00:55:28.980 --> 00:55:34.860
And that's the control that
you enact on your system.

00:55:34.860 --> 00:55:38.460
Then the optimization problem
is repeated at time t plus 1,

00:55:38.460 --> 00:55:41.420
but with a new state constraint
that the state predicted

00:55:41.420 --> 00:55:44.260
at time t plus 1 from
the perspective of t

00:55:44.260 --> 00:55:47.760
plus 1 has to be the state at
time t plus 1, as measured.

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:54.940
So now, we can define
a closed-loop policy

00:55:54.940 --> 00:55:59.820
as the first control
input that is determined

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:01.940
when we solve the optimal
control problem starting

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:03.580
from the state x of t.

00:56:03.580 --> 00:56:06.340
You see how, actually, this
is how we are closing the loop

00:56:06.340 --> 00:56:10.530
and bridging the gap between
closed-loop control--

00:56:10.530 --> 00:56:11.690
Sorry.

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:14.170
--and the open-loop
optimal control.

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:15.530
Is this point clear?

00:56:15.530 --> 00:56:20.010
That's really the crux of MPC.

00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:22.210
So then the closed-loop
evolves as xt plus 1

00:56:22.210 --> 00:56:24.890
equals Ax plus B
pi, which we can

00:56:24.890 --> 00:56:27.970
abstractly written as a
closed-loop function of x

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:28.790
and the time.

00:56:31.930 --> 00:56:34.050
So how do we characterize
the closed-loop behavior

00:56:34.050 --> 00:56:37.490
of the system?

00:56:37.490 --> 00:56:40.750
One more thing notationally,
and I know this is a bit boring,

00:56:40.750 --> 00:56:45.130
but it will be needed
later, is that in the case

00:56:45.130 --> 00:56:47.850
that the problem
is time-invariant,

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:51.990
that is, A does not explicitly
depend on the current time,

00:56:51.990 --> 00:56:55.570
so there is no
subscript k, B does not

00:56:55.570 --> 00:56:58.010
depend on the current
time, C does not

00:56:58.010 --> 00:57:00.730
depend on the current time.

00:57:00.730 --> 00:57:02.850
So this problem,
the only thing that

00:57:02.850 --> 00:57:06.770
changes across planning horizon
is the initial condition

00:57:06.770 --> 00:57:08.120
for the state.

00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:13.360
So we can write it in
a simpler form, where

00:57:13.360 --> 00:57:17.360
we drop all the
things of predicting

00:57:17.360 --> 00:57:20.080
from perspective of time
t and so on and so forth.

00:57:20.080 --> 00:57:23.240
We can just drop
all that terminology

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:26.020
and use this
simpler formulation.

00:57:26.020 --> 00:57:29.080
But we're understanding that
we solve this problem starting

00:57:29.080 --> 00:57:30.280
at state x of t.

00:57:30.280 --> 00:57:32.440
We use the first control u0.

00:57:32.440 --> 00:57:34.528
We transition into a
new state xt plus 1.

00:57:34.528 --> 00:57:36.320
And then we solve the
same problem, but now

00:57:36.320 --> 00:57:39.240
with x0 equal xt plus 1.

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:40.740
Yeah.

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:42.600
[INAUDIBLE]

00:57:42.600 --> 00:57:43.340
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:57:43.340 --> 00:57:46.440
So does this formulation
require that our time

00:57:46.440 --> 00:57:51.480
horizon is long enough for
the final state at time N or--

00:57:51.480 --> 00:57:53.600
Yeah, yeah, otherwise,
you will not get there.

00:57:53.600 --> 00:57:54.100
Yes.

00:57:54.100 --> 00:57:55.360
Yeah, yeah.

00:57:55.360 --> 00:58:00.920
I mean, in general,
the best way to see

00:58:00.920 --> 00:58:05.470
MPC is that the underlying
problem you're trying to solve

00:58:05.470 --> 00:58:09.010
is an infinite horizon,
which, as we discussed before,

00:58:09.010 --> 00:58:12.570
is a bit of an abstraction,
of course, was a good framing.

00:58:15.860 --> 00:58:19.370
Make it so if your
horizon is shorter,

00:58:19.370 --> 00:58:22.250
you might have more
aggressive control?

00:58:22.250 --> 00:58:22.970
Yeah, it will be.

00:58:22.970 --> 00:58:23.470
OK.

00:58:23.470 --> 00:58:26.150
So actually, if
it's too short, you

00:58:26.150 --> 00:58:29.190
may not actually converge
and regulate your state.

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:34.910
So that's why the time
horizon, the terminal cost, p,

00:58:34.910 --> 00:58:37.750
and the terminal
constraint, x of f,

00:58:37.750 --> 00:58:43.510
are crucial to tune to make
sure that you properly balance

00:58:43.510 --> 00:58:46.830
lookahead capabilities
or how long in the future

00:58:46.830 --> 00:58:51.090
you optimize over, which is, in
general, the longer the better.

00:58:51.090 --> 00:58:53.030
You make your
decision on your form,

00:58:53.030 --> 00:58:56.030
but you pay more
computational cost.

00:58:56.030 --> 00:58:59.270
And how short your time
horizon is, in which case

00:58:59.270 --> 00:59:02.430
you might be too greedy or
even myopic, in which case,

00:59:02.430 --> 00:59:04.100
you may not fulfill your task.

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:06.740
So it's going to be a
delicate balance between how

00:59:06.740 --> 00:59:10.420
much computation time you want
to invest versus whether you

00:59:10.420 --> 00:59:11.920
can actually achieve your task.

00:59:18.380 --> 00:59:19.980
OK, so let's simplify
the notation.

00:59:19.980 --> 00:59:23.900
Then your optimal control
is the first control input

00:59:23.900 --> 00:59:26.880
that you compute in the previous
optimal control formulation,

00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:31.820
and then you have a
closed-loop control law.

00:59:31.820 --> 00:59:35.340
Now, what type of cost
functions do people use?

00:59:35.340 --> 00:59:38.060
Here, there are
not many surprises.

00:59:38.060 --> 00:59:40.380
So for the running
cost, quite common

00:59:40.380 --> 00:59:43.540
to use just the quadratic
cost similar to LQR.

00:59:43.540 --> 00:59:48.640
Or you might use also some other
p norms, where for example,

00:59:48.640 --> 00:59:52.520
you could use the 1-norm
or the infinity-norm.

00:59:58.060 --> 00:59:59.640
The 1-norm of the vector--

01:00:04.650 --> 01:00:05.810
I don't have a marker.

01:00:12.410 --> 01:00:18.290
--is the sum of the absolute
value of its component instead

01:00:18.290 --> 01:00:19.030
of the square.

01:00:24.210 --> 01:00:29.010
So to wrap it up, in online
model predictive control,

01:00:29.010 --> 01:00:31.730
we measure the state x of t.

01:00:31.730 --> 01:00:35.770
We solve finite time
optimal control problem.

01:00:35.770 --> 01:00:40.670
If, god forbid, the problem
isn't infeasible, you're stuck,

01:00:40.670 --> 01:00:41.930
you call for help.

01:00:41.930 --> 01:00:45.550
Otherwise, you apply the first
element U0 to the system.

01:00:45.550 --> 01:00:48.410
You transition to the
new time t plus 1.

01:00:48.410 --> 01:00:50.230
You measure xt plus 1.

01:00:50.230 --> 01:00:52.610
And you repeat.

01:00:52.610 --> 01:00:53.948
Why online?

01:00:53.948 --> 01:00:55.490
Because here, the
idea is that we are

01:00:55.490 --> 01:00:57.710
solving this problem online.

01:00:57.710 --> 01:01:00.090
So as you are
deploying your system.

01:01:00.090 --> 01:01:01.910
As we will discuss
briefly next time,

01:01:01.910 --> 01:01:04.250
there are also
offline formulation,

01:01:04.250 --> 01:01:07.410
whereby you do the
optimization offline,

01:01:07.410 --> 01:01:10.050
basically anticipating all
possible conditions that

01:01:10.050 --> 01:01:12.210
might arise.

01:01:12.210 --> 01:01:15.550
But for most of these
exposition of MPC,

01:01:15.550 --> 01:01:20.116
we will consider only
the online version.

01:01:20.116 --> 01:01:25.410
Right, so what I was mentioning
before is what are the things

01:01:25.410 --> 01:01:26.350
we care about?

01:01:26.350 --> 01:01:34.010
So the two things we care about
are persistent feasibility

01:01:34.010 --> 01:01:35.530
and stability.

01:01:35.530 --> 01:01:36.510
Persistent feasibility.

01:01:36.510 --> 01:01:39.730
We want to make sure
that the optimal control

01:01:39.730 --> 01:01:42.490
problem is always feasible.

01:01:42.490 --> 01:01:44.450
That's quite obvious.

01:01:44.450 --> 01:01:48.010
Stability means that
we want to make sure

01:01:48.010 --> 01:01:51.810
that the genetic control
inputs lead to trajectories

01:01:51.810 --> 01:01:54.490
that converge to the origin.

01:01:54.490 --> 01:01:58.320
So the question is, how do
we tune the three main design

01:01:58.320 --> 01:02:02.240
knobs so that these two
objectives are fulfilled?

01:02:02.240 --> 01:02:04.742
Up until the '90s, this
was mostly heuristic.

01:02:04.742 --> 01:02:07.200
Then between the '90s and 2000,
that's where people started

01:02:07.200 --> 01:02:10.400
putting some theory to allow you
to make informed choices about

01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:12.680
how to select
those tuning knobs.

01:02:12.680 --> 01:02:16.313
So-- sorry for the cough.

01:02:16.313 --> 01:02:17.980
That's what we're
going to discuss next.

01:02:21.800 --> 01:02:25.300
So there are two possible ways
of approaching the problem.

01:02:25.300 --> 01:02:29.140
The first one is a
closed-loop system, right?

01:02:29.140 --> 01:02:37.800
So we have xt plus 1 equal
f for a closed-loop x of t.

01:02:37.800 --> 01:02:41.760
We try to somewhat
analyze how the dynamics

01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:44.560
of this closed-loop system
evolve depending on the three

01:02:44.560 --> 01:02:47.760
tuning knobs, and then we
optimize the three tuning knobs.

01:02:47.760 --> 01:02:53.240
That's very hard because this
is a non-linear system that

01:02:53.240 --> 01:02:55.890
entails an inner optimization.

01:02:55.890 --> 01:02:59.150
So basically, this is
a closed-loop function

01:02:59.150 --> 01:03:01.970
f is the result of applying
an optimization step.

01:03:01.970 --> 01:03:03.670
So it's difficult to analyze.

01:03:03.670 --> 01:03:06.693
And that's where the
people were stuck.

01:03:06.693 --> 01:03:08.110
Then people realized
that a better

01:03:08.110 --> 01:03:11.070
way to do that is to
directly derive conditions

01:03:11.070 --> 01:03:14.790
in the terminal function p the
terminal constraint set Xf,

01:03:14.790 --> 01:03:17.590
so that the closed-loop
feasibility are guaranteed

01:03:17.590 --> 01:03:24.230
by design without having to
analyze ad hoc the trajectory

01:03:24.230 --> 01:03:26.190
evolutions.

01:03:26.190 --> 01:03:27.910
So what we're
going to do next is

01:03:27.910 --> 01:03:31.210
to first tackle the problem
of persistent feasibility.

01:03:31.210 --> 01:03:32.730
That's the most basic one.

01:03:32.730 --> 01:03:35.070
If you can even solve the
optimal control problem,

01:03:35.070 --> 01:03:37.390
then the question of stability
is not even relevant.

01:03:37.390 --> 01:03:39.943
And then we're going
to see how we can--

01:03:39.943 --> 01:03:42.110
once we solve the problem
of persistent feasibility,

01:03:42.110 --> 01:03:45.390
address the problem
of stability.

01:03:45.390 --> 01:03:48.070
So let's focus on persistent
feasibility first.

01:03:48.070 --> 01:03:51.310
So the goal is to
tune our tuning knobs

01:03:51.310 --> 01:03:55.540
so that feasibility for all
future times is guaranteed.

01:03:55.540 --> 01:03:57.660
To address a problem,
we leverage tools

01:03:57.660 --> 01:03:59.468
from invariant set theory.

01:03:59.468 --> 01:04:01.760
So I'm going to give you a
little bit more terminology.

01:04:01.760 --> 01:04:04.260
Sorry about that.

01:04:04.260 --> 01:04:10.800
MPC is a bit less mathematically
intense than reachability,

01:04:10.800 --> 01:04:14.860
but it's a bit more boring
in terms of terminology.

01:04:14.860 --> 01:04:16.600
What is invariant set?

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:19.840
So let me first pin down a
little bit of terminology.

01:04:19.840 --> 01:04:24.060
So we define the set of
feasible initial state capital

01:04:24.060 --> 01:04:28.960
X0 as the set of states
that are feasible,

01:04:28.960 --> 01:04:32.860
meaning belong to the
set capital X, such

01:04:32.860 --> 01:04:37.620
that there is a feasible
controlled sequence.

01:04:37.620 --> 01:04:43.380
By feasible meaning that each
control Uk belongs to capital U,

01:04:43.380 --> 01:04:45.700
such that the
evolution of the state

01:04:45.700 --> 01:04:48.460
trajectories stemming from an
application of this control

01:04:48.460 --> 01:04:52.170
sequence gives
rise to states that

01:04:52.170 --> 01:04:56.170
are feasible at
all times and such

01:04:56.170 --> 01:05:02.490
that the terminal state belongs
to the desired final set.

01:05:02.490 --> 01:05:04.810
So this is a set of
initial conditions

01:05:04.810 --> 01:05:06.890
for which the optimal
control problem

01:05:06.890 --> 01:05:10.690
has at least one solution.

01:05:10.690 --> 01:05:14.890
A control input can be found
only if our current state

01:05:14.890 --> 01:05:16.390
belongs to this set.

01:05:19.450 --> 01:05:25.170
Then we define a notion
of a controllable set.

01:05:25.170 --> 01:05:27.290
For an autonomous
systems xt plus 1

01:05:27.290 --> 01:05:31.370
equal to phi of x of t with
state and control constraints,

01:05:31.370 --> 01:05:34.690
the one-step
controllable set to set S

01:05:34.690 --> 01:05:37.850
is defined as the
set of states such

01:05:37.850 --> 01:05:42.770
that after one-step application
of our transition dynamics,

01:05:42.770 --> 01:05:47.870
we end up into
set S. Yes, great.

01:05:47.870 --> 01:05:50.330
Thank you.

01:05:50.330 --> 01:06:09.890
Specifically-- say
that you're given a set

01:06:09.890 --> 01:06:13.450
S. The question
that you are asking

01:06:13.450 --> 01:06:20.930
is, what is the set of the
states, referred to as Pre

01:06:20.930 --> 01:06:27.810
or previous, of S, such
that if you start here

01:06:27.810 --> 01:06:32.170
and then you apply your
dynamics, f of x, you

01:06:32.170 --> 01:06:34.730
end up here?

01:06:34.730 --> 01:06:37.970
So given a set, you're trying
to answer the question, what

01:06:37.970 --> 01:06:40.970
is the set of k such
that after one-step

01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:45.750
under the natural dynamics
of the system, I land into S?

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:52.160
The notion of a one-step
controllable set

01:06:52.160 --> 01:06:55.020
extends to the case
of control system,

01:06:55.020 --> 01:06:58.280
where now xt plus 1 is
a function of x and u,

01:06:58.280 --> 01:07:01.200
where the definition
is a very similar.

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:04.680
The only difference
is that now, we

01:07:04.680 --> 01:07:07.120
define the one-step
controllable set

01:07:07.120 --> 01:07:10.200
as the set of states for
which there exists a u, such

01:07:10.200 --> 01:07:15.680
that a one-step application of
our dynamics leads us into S.

01:07:15.680 --> 01:07:21.240
So there exists a
u such that f of xu

01:07:21.240 --> 01:07:24.240
leads me into S in one step.

01:07:24.240 --> 01:07:25.980
Well, that's just
the definition.

01:07:28.560 --> 01:07:30.260
One more set of definitions.

01:07:30.260 --> 01:07:34.800
So another important
definition is the notion

01:07:34.800 --> 01:07:37.300
of a control invariant set.

01:07:37.300 --> 01:07:39.240
Actually, this is a
concept that arises

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:44.800
quite ubiquitously in
model predictive control.

01:07:44.800 --> 01:07:48.650
Given set C is a
control invariant set--

01:07:58.390 --> 01:08:03.110
Let's call it C. Given set
C is a control invariant set

01:08:03.110 --> 01:08:06.550
for a given system xt
plus 1 equal f of xu.

01:08:11.070 --> 01:08:15.990
If you start in C-- so
this is your state, x of t.

01:08:15.990 --> 01:08:21.270
--then there exists a control
u such that under the state

01:08:21.270 --> 01:08:25.830
transition dynamics,
you land in C.

01:08:25.830 --> 01:08:27.649
So if you start
with C, think of C,

01:08:27.649 --> 01:08:31.029
say, as the safe set, the
set of relative states

01:08:31.029 --> 01:08:32.990
where you are far
enough from danger,

01:08:32.990 --> 01:08:36.170
there exists a u that allows
you to stay in that set.

01:08:36.170 --> 01:08:39.109
It seems a useful concept.

01:08:39.109 --> 01:08:48.500
So there exists a u
such that f of xu lands

01:08:48.500 --> 01:08:52.939
into, again, C, the same set.

01:08:52.939 --> 01:08:54.700
So of course, we
want to choose the C

01:08:54.700 --> 01:08:59.220
as a set of desirable states.

01:08:59.220 --> 01:09:02.540
The set C infinity-- this is
really just a minor notation.

01:09:02.540 --> 01:09:04.819
--is said to be the
maximum control invariant

01:09:04.819 --> 01:09:07.899
set if this control invariant
contains all control

01:09:07.899 --> 01:09:09.240
invariant sets containing x.

01:09:09.240 --> 01:09:11.448
It's basically the largest
possible control invariant

01:09:11.448 --> 01:09:15.500
set that you might have
for a dynamical system.

01:09:15.500 --> 01:09:20.600
Similar definition arises
for autonomous systems.

01:09:20.600 --> 01:09:23.939
So systems where you don't
have explicit dependency

01:09:23.939 --> 01:09:24.740
on the control.

01:09:24.740 --> 01:09:29.979
The set is said to be
positive invariant if,

01:09:29.979 --> 01:09:33.180
after one application of the--

01:09:33.180 --> 01:09:37.720
so if you start in the set A
and you apply your dynamics,

01:09:37.720 --> 01:09:43.450
you still remain in A.

01:09:43.450 --> 01:09:47.010
So these sets can be computed by
using a fairly powerful Matlab

01:09:47.010 --> 01:09:48.350
tool called MPT.

01:09:48.350 --> 01:09:51.029
And actually, you will use this
tool as part of your homework.

01:09:51.029 --> 01:09:51.529
Yeah.

01:09:51.529 --> 01:09:55.410
Can you explain how a control
invariant set could not

01:09:55.410 --> 01:09:57.090
be maximal?

01:09:57.090 --> 01:10:00.090
Like a good example
of a set that--

01:10:00.090 --> 01:10:02.250
Yeah, I mean, sure.

01:10:02.250 --> 01:10:04.450
I mean, you might have
a control invariant set

01:10:04.450 --> 01:10:08.210
with this definition,
but no one is telling us

01:10:08.210 --> 01:10:14.970
that there might not
be an extension that

01:10:14.970 --> 01:10:16.630
still has this property.

01:10:16.630 --> 01:10:18.870
So actually, you
must-- so for example,

01:10:18.870 --> 01:10:22.650
a control invariant
set could be 0 for--

01:10:22.650 --> 01:10:25.490
I mean, literally the set
with a single point, 0,

01:10:25.490 --> 01:10:27.950
whereby if you have
a linear system

01:10:27.950 --> 01:10:32.170
and you start a state equals
0, you use control u equals 0,

01:10:32.170 --> 01:10:34.890
the next state is 0.

01:10:34.890 --> 01:10:37.050
So the origin typically--

01:10:37.050 --> 01:10:38.070
you see what I'm saying?

01:10:38.070 --> 01:10:41.640
So you start at the
origin, x equals 0.

01:10:41.640 --> 01:10:44.800
Assuming that the control
u is a feasible control,

01:10:44.800 --> 01:10:49.100
then Ax plus Bu is still 0,
which means the next state is 0.

01:10:49.100 --> 01:10:50.800
So that's a control
invariant set.

01:10:50.800 --> 01:10:54.240
But that doesn't mean that there
might be some other states that

01:10:54.240 --> 01:11:01.080
actually still allow you to
transition into the same set.

01:11:01.080 --> 01:11:05.160
We'll see actually
some examples later.

01:11:05.160 --> 01:11:11.160
All right, so let's get to the
first useful result related

01:11:11.160 --> 01:11:15.760
to feasibility,
which is as follows.

01:11:15.760 --> 01:11:20.520
So let's define x1 as the
truncated feasibility set.

01:11:20.520 --> 01:11:27.320
So the set of states for
which the problem now

01:11:27.320 --> 01:11:31.720
solves over the horizon
N minus 1 is feasible.

01:11:31.720 --> 01:11:34.160
So this is the set of states
such that there exists

01:11:34.160 --> 01:11:37.000
a truncated control
sequence starting from u1

01:11:37.000 --> 01:11:42.960
and not u0 such
that it is feasible.

01:11:42.960 --> 01:11:44.457
So Uk belongs to u.

01:11:44.457 --> 01:11:46.540
And so that under the
application of the dynamics,

01:11:46.540 --> 01:11:48.880
the states satisfies
the state constraints

01:11:48.880 --> 01:11:52.240
and satisfies the
terminal constraint.

01:11:52.240 --> 01:11:53.880
The key result is as follows.

01:11:53.880 --> 01:12:01.720
If set x1 is a control
invariant set for your system,

01:12:01.720 --> 01:12:06.260
then the MPC law is
persistently feasible.

01:12:09.400 --> 01:12:11.060
The intuition is
that, basically,

01:12:11.060 --> 01:12:15.760
if x1 is control invariant, you
can use this control invariant

01:12:15.760 --> 01:12:18.820
to buy a one-step of
staying in that set,

01:12:18.820 --> 01:12:23.520
and then solve the problem
for N minus 1 steps.

01:12:23.520 --> 01:12:25.080
Now, the result per se--

01:12:25.080 --> 01:12:27.240
I mean, we'll prove
this result next time.

01:12:27.240 --> 01:12:30.800
The result per se is
completely useless

01:12:30.800 --> 01:12:36.910
because your tuning knobs
are Xf at the terminal cost p

01:12:36.910 --> 01:12:41.350
and at the horizon N. You
don't really control--

01:12:41.350 --> 01:12:44.110
you don't really tune x1.

01:12:44.110 --> 01:12:47.070
But as we will see,
this is a useful lemma

01:12:47.070 --> 01:12:51.750
that allows us to then derive
a theorem that is actually

01:12:51.750 --> 01:12:52.330
actionable.

01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:56.430
Allows us to tune the tuning
knobs that we have in MPC.

01:12:56.430 --> 01:12:59.070
So we'll prove this
lemma next time.

01:12:59.070 --> 01:13:01.510
Take 30 minutes at most.

01:13:01.510 --> 01:13:03.230
And then we continue
our exposition

01:13:03.230 --> 01:13:06.950
about persistent feasibility,
and then we move into stability.

01:13:06.950 --> 01:13:09.910
The end result will be a set
of conditions that tells you

01:13:09.910 --> 01:13:15.670
explicitly how to tune p
and terminal set X of f

01:13:15.670 --> 01:13:17.850
to ensure persistent
feasibility and stability.

01:13:17.850 --> 01:13:21.590
And then your p set, you
will solve a few problems

01:13:21.590 --> 01:13:27.310
so that you really
appreciate this theorem.

01:13:27.310 --> 01:13:29.270
All right?

01:13:29.270 --> 01:13:30.820
OK.

